ure. Let's see.\n\nAnother angle: the melds of other players. The previous player has pungs of W3 and W5. The next player has a pung of W4. The player across has a pung of T7-T8-T9. So they might be going for some patterns. If I discard a tile that they need, they might claim it, but since the action is to play a tile, maybe I should avoid giving them tiles they need.\n\nBut how to know? For example, if I discard T9, the player across has a pung of T7-T8-T9, so they might have already taken T9. But their melds show T7-T8-T9 as a pung? Wait, no, the melds of the player sitting across are [[T7, T8, T9]], which is a chow, not a pung. So they have a chow of T7-T8-T9. So if I discard T9, they might have already used it in their chow, so maybe it's safe? Or maybe they need another T9 for something else.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W8, the next player has a pung of W4 and played W5, W8. The previous player has pungs of W3 and W5. So maybe W8 is not needed by them.\n\nAlternatively, looking at my own hand, perhaps discarding a tile that allows me to form a winning hand later. For example, if I discard W8, then my remaining tiles are W1x2, W9x2, T1, T6, T9, B1, B2, B8, B9, F4x2. Let's see if this can form a winning hand. If I can draw a tile to complete a pattern.\n\nBut since I can't draw in this step, maybe the next step is to play a tile that leaves me with a hand that can easily form a winning pattern. For example, if I discard W8, then I have more terminals and honors. Let's see:\n\nAfter discarding W8, the tiles are:\n\nW1x2, W9x2, T1, T6, T9, B1, B2, B8, B9, F4x2.\n\nStill have T6 (non-terminal), B2 (non-terminal), B8 (non-terminal). So All Terminals and Honors is not possible yet. Maybe if I can get rid of T6, B2, B8. But that's three tiles. So perhaps the next step is to discard one of them.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, the options include Play T6, B2, B8, W8.\n\nIf I discard T6, then my hand has:\n\nW1x2, W8, W9x2, T1, T9, B1, B2, B8, B9, F4x2.\n\nStill have